00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:01.750 align:start position:0%
 
the tech industry is crashing and  unfortunately there isn't a simple  answer as to why this has happened  before in the year 2000 during the  dot-com bubble but everything is  different this time around this downturn  is affecting nearly everyone tech stocks  are way down but so are traditional  retailers and even though bitcoin was  supposed to be protected against  inflation it's fallen over 50  but there are a few people who have  spent years preparing for this and i'm  not just talking about warren buffett  who's been focused on value stocks for  his entire career a small segment of the  market is doing just fine but the  question is why what do this year's high  performers see that we're all missing  and how did today's billionaires get  through previous financial downturns in  order to answer these questions we have  to analyze how we got here the last two  years have led to absolutely insane  growth for tech stocks the nasdaq more  than doubled venture capital investment  also doubled and the number of unicorn  startups worth more than a billion  dollars increased to over 1 000 tech was  already growing really quickly over the  last decade but 2020 and 2021  accelerated things to an absolute frenzy  there were four key drivers of this  recent growth first we saw a real  technological shift over the past decade  as cloud computing became ubiquitous and  software worked its way into every  aspect of personal and business life ten  years ago most companies use technology  in some capacity to manage their  businesses but recently it's become  clear that any company without a solid  technology strategy is dead in the water  this shift has led to hundreds of large  technology companies going public and  everyone was predicting that this trend  would continue but in previous business  cycles successful tech companies would  go public much earlier in their growth  that's changed and for the last few  years we've seen tech companies stay  private for much longer this trend has  led to more venture capital dollars  flowing into these late stage tech  startups without the public markets to  act as a check on company financials  valuations have soared these high  valuations were driven in part by low  interest rates since the us government  has kept interest rates so low investors  have gone hunting for new ways to earn  decent returns they can't just park a  billion dollars in bonds and make  reasonable money so they have to take  more risk on tech startups the low  interest rate environment created a glut  of capital that worked its way into tech  companies but now interest rates are  rising and every company is getting  repriced the last driver of the recent  tech surge was the covet effect when the  pandemic first hit venture capitalists  worried that there would be a severe  recession and startups would have  trouble raising more money sequoia  capital even published a memo saying  that this was a black swan event and  things were going to be incredibly  difficult for the foreseeable future but  then something unexpected happened the  us government stepped in with the  largest stimulus package in history and  ended the recession incredibly quickly  at the same time tech products like zoom  started to look indispensable so  investors started piling money into  anything that could be used remotely  travel companies still took a hit as did  anything related to in-person events but  overall things looked really good for  tech people were shopping online more  which led to massive growth for shopify  working out at home became extremely  popular so peloton took off and with  movie theaters closed indefinitely  netflix became the go-to place for  at-home entertainment there was a  problem though see after a few months of  adjustment everyone thought these  changes would be permanent the pandemic  had led to massive adoption of all these  tech products and there was no sign that  it would slow down but it did just look  at this graph of e-commerce shopping the  percentage of shopping activity that  happens online had been growing steadily  for decades from 2000 all the way to  2020 the graph is incredibly smooth but  then all of a sudden the pandemic hit  and e-commerce adoption spiked from 11  to nearly 16 but as things opened back  up people went back to their old habits  and started shopping in person more this  phenomenon is called reversion to the  mean and it's an incredibly powerful  force when looking at long-term trends  whenever there is a big market move  people rush to point to one single  reason to explain everything but that's  a trap the real story here is much more  complicated and it's better to think  about this as a sequence of events covet  hits in 2020 everyone thinks there will  be a massive recession but then the  government steps in with a huge stimulus  and low interest rates that puts dollars  in everyone's pockets consumers go out  and buy things and investors pour money  into companies but that creates a  problem see manufacturing capacity got  hit really hard during covid people were  sick and couldn't show up to factories  to make things so supply dropped and  what happens when supply drops while the  money supply increases demand that's  right inflation so now the government  has a new problem on their hands  inflation hurts everyone and you don't  need to look further than your local gas  station to see that prices are rising  and long-term inflation can create  reinforcing spirals that erode the value  of the us dollar the government doesn't  want that so they have to fight back the  answer is rising interest rates but this  hurts tech stocks the most stocks are  typically valued based on their future  cash flows and most tech companies  aren't generating a lot of cash flow  right now they're in growth mode working  hard to develop new products and scale  their businesses this is fine when  interest rates are low but as rates rise  they get hurt the most and that's what  we're seeing in the market currently the  nasdaq is down nearly 30 percent but we  need to dig in deeper to understand  what's really going on see not all  companies on the nasdaq are created  equal some are profitable and some  aren't and it's the unprofitable  companies that have been hit the hardest  at the end of last year unprofitable  tech companies were trading at 25 times  revenue but now they trade at just six  times revenue this collapse has already  hurt tech investors a ton but the real  question is what happens next in order  to answer that it's useful to look at  what happened during previous market  sell-offs see it's easy to watch a bunch  of high-flying tech companies fall in  value and immediately say oh this is  just like the dot-com bubble all over  again but that's not quite right in the  year 2000 basically any tech company  could go public on pure hype alone they  didn't need profits they didn't need  revenue and sometimes they didn't even  need a successful product that's what  happened with a company called mp3.com  that was one of the first attempts at  online music streaming in july of 1999  mp3.com went public at a valuation of 4  billion  the internet was extremely trendy and  online music streaming seemed like an  inevitability the only problem was that  the company was still extremely small by  all accounts at the peak of mp3.com the  company had less than 1 million daily  active users and was only making a few  million dollars in revenue per year now  let's compare that to spotify spotify  has over 422 million active users and  brings in nearly 10 billion in revenue  per year and yet while mp3.com was  valued at 4 billion  spotify is worth only 20 billion dollars  today so spotify has 400 times the  amount of users and over a thousand  times as much revenue but they're only  worth five times as much you can clearly  see how overvalued.com stocks were back  in 2000 so what's the point of this  comparison well it's meant to illustrate  that the tech companies we have today  are a lot more established than two  decades ago most of them make billions  of dollars and are actually used by  hundreds of millions of people music  streaming is clearly a valuable service  so even though the market may be  re-evaluating the appropriate price for  the company they clearly have built a  real business unlike what we saw during  the dot-com boom there's a good reason  why people are calling this the great  revaluation the current crop of tech  companies are real businesses they just  might be overvalued which is leading to  this massive sell-off now there's  another interesting lesson from the  dot-com crash we should dig into  amazon.com as the name implies the great  revaluation is all about how the market  values companies revenue multiples are a  big part of that for every dollar a  business generates in revenue how much  should that be worth to an investor  remember mp3.com was trading at more  than a thousand times revenue during the  dot-com bubble whereas spotify trades at  two times revenue today well jeff bezos  went through his own frothy period  during the dot-com boom at its peak  right before the turn of the millennium  amazon was trading at more than 55 times  revenue then the market tanked and even  though amazon has grown significantly  the revenue multiple has stayed constant  around three times revenue it's almost  as if investors went through a period of  price discovery where they really had no  idea how to value amazon but then all of  a sudden it clicked and the multiple has  been stable for the past two decades now  today we're seeing something very  similar happen with shopify the revenue  multiple has been all over the place in  2016 shopify was trading for around 10  times revenue then it spiked to over 60  times revenue during the pandemic and  now it's back down to around 10 times  revenue looking at the graph you can see  that investors still don't know how to  properly value shopify's stock and  that's a big part of the reason why  shopify's ceo toby lucky took to twitter  to ask if financial analysts are being  held accountable for their predictions  for another interesting historical  example let's look at the online pet  food industry back in 1999 there were  not one but four online pet food  companies that raised significant  funding petopia.com petsmart.com  pets.com and petstore.com all raised  tens of millions of dollars in 1999.  then in february of 2000 pets.com raised  82 million dollars more in an ipo by the  end of the year they had managed to  spend over a hundred million dollars  including two million dollars for a  super bowl ad and wind up bankrupt none  of those four companies generated  significant revenues but today we're  seeing something different chewy.com  seems to have figured out how to sell  pet food online because they sell a lot  of it nine billion dollars just in 2021  but even though their revenues have been  growing pretty steadily chewy's  valuation has been all over the place  during the pandemic they traded at  almost five times revenue now they're  back to just over one times revenue the  high expectations that covid would  permanently change consumer behavior are  coming crashing down to earth and with  it so are the valuations of these  companies a big part of why tech  companies could fetch sky-high  valuations historically has been the  fact that they capitalized on either  moore's law metcalfe's law or both  moore's law essentially says that  computers double in power every two  years while metcalfe's law says that  networks get exponentially more valuable  as they grow in the early days of  facebook they were kind of writing both  of these trends computers were getting  faster and cheaper so people were  spending more and more time online and  at the same time facebook was a network  that perfectly benefited from metcalf's  law every time someone signed up for  facebook the whole site got more  valuable because there was more content  on it but moore's law has been slowing  down for years now and many new tech  companies just simply don't have network  effects so these companies are grappling  with the painful realization that they  might not be able to justify as high of  revenue multiples as they have in the  past in 2008 the global financial crisis  caused a deep recession and a massive  market sell-off driven primarily by a  housing bubble and an intertwined  derivatives market the crisis didn't  affect tech all that much but it can  still be useful to look at as a guide  today just as the market was collapsing  sequoia capital released the now famous  r.i.p good times memo in the 56 slide  deck they cover dozens of different  macroeconomic indicators that help  explain what's going on things like  personal savings rates unemployment and  inflation were all key factors in  driving the last recession so the  question is how do we stack up today  well there's some good news and there's  some bad news first the good  unemployment is currently lower than it  was before the 2008 recession and  personal saving rates are also higher  before the housing crisis the average  u.s household was spending 13 of their  income servicing debt today that number  has fallen to 9 percent so overall more  people have jobs and households have  less debt good stuff the bad news is  that inflation is currently much higher  than it was going into the 2008  recession and that means that the  government will have fewer ways to  respond to the downturn the us  government also has way more debt than  it did back in 2008. actually it's twice  as much on a debt to gdp basis that  means that big bailout packages will  probably be harder to justify this time  around but there is some cause for  optimism as rough as the 2008 downturn  was innovation didn't slow down great  companies still get built regardless of  market conditions and that's what we see  time and time again in 2008 small  startups like airbnb square and stripe  all got going and made it through and in  the dot-com bubble google paypal and  amazon.com all found a way to persevere  even while the stock market was down 80  or more warren buffett is famous for  saying only when the tide goes out do  you discover who's been swimming naked  and i suspect we will see a lot of that  over the next few years startups that  haven't really found a way to deliver a  clear value to a customer will wash out  but the best companies will survive and  that's cause for optimism anyone who's  been in the startup world for more than  a few years can tell you how crazy 2020  and 2021 have been money has been flying  around like crazy and it was pretty  clear that it couldn't keep accelerating  forever but the question remains where  will the venture capital industry land  in a year or two after the dust settles  will it look like 2008 where vc  investing decreased by 30 percent or  will it look more like the dot-com  bubble where vc investing dropped by 87  well clearly a dot-com crash style  pullback would be terrible but if that  did happen the vc industry would only  reset to 2012 levels and that's actually  when i got my start in silicon valley it  wasn't easy to raise money for a startup  back in 2012 but it wasn't impossible my  first funding round was just seventeen  thousand dollars and i had to live in a  single bedroom in a rundown house that i  shared with my co-founder to stretch  that cash but i still got to work on  interesting problems and it ultimately  led me to where i am today even though  startups weren't able to raise millions  of dollars with nothing more than a  pitch deck back then it really wasn't  that bad i had enough money to pay rent  and it gave me all the time i needed to  learn to program some people who are  only interested in startups or  technology for the money will wash out  and go get other jobs but for the truly  dedicated builders nothing can stop them  so if you want an inspiring story of an  entrepreneur who stuck it out for  decades during good times and bad just  go watch my video on epic games next

